commit 25ef620381ca758d5f58e4749373f74138e60a33
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Jul 17 22:14:03 2026 +0200

    VoxtralRealtime streaming: stop clearing the Metal buffer pool on every step (#229)
    
    * Stop clearing the Metal buffer pool on every streaming step
    
    VoxtralRealtimeStreamSession.advance() called Memory.clearCache() on both
    exit paths, i.e. once per audio chunk (10x/s at a 100 ms feed cadence).
    Emptying the pool that often forces every step to re-allocate its whole
    working set cold from the Metal driver and makes any Memory.cacheLimit a
    caller sets ineffective; in live dictation it shows up as a 10 Hz RSS
    sawtooth (weights floor <-> per-step transient peak) and a per-step
    allocation tax.
    
    Match the offline generate() loop instead: clear every 256 decoded tokens
    inside the decode loop, plus once when the utterance finishes so memory
    returns to the weight floor between sessions. Hosts that want a hard bound
    on pool growth set Memory.cacheLimit (mirrors mlx-lm/voxmlx practice of
    set_cache_limit + rare clears).
    
    * Correct the finish-time clearCache comment: it cannot reach the weight floor alone
    
    The final clear runs while the session's KV caches and carried front-end
    state are still live; they land in the buffer pool when the caller releases
    the session, so idle memory settles at cacheLimit, not the weight floor.
    Field-measured in localvoxtral (2026-07-17): ~5 GB resident between
    utterances at a 2 GB cap until the host cleared again after the release.
    Document the caller-side contract instead of promising the floor.
    
    * Scope the finish-time clear comment to finish(): EOS-ended utterances skip it
    
    * Tighten the cache-cadence comment

