commit 3fa030371557d56113ebe2aeb78cc500c989f315
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Jul 17 22:13:31 2026 +0200

    VoxtralRealtime streaming: incremental mel/conv front end (O(N²) → O(N) per utterance) (#230)
    
    * Add incremental mel + conv-stem primitives for Voxtral Realtime streaming
    
    VoxtralRealtimeStreamSession currently recomputes the whole front end
    (reflect pad, STFT/mel, both causal convs) over the entire accumulated
    buffer on every step, which is O(N^2) per utterance. These primitives
    compute the same values incrementally:
    
    * VoxtralRealtimeMelStream carries the not-yet-framed suffix of the
      padded sample stream between calls and emits a mel column only once
      its 400-sample window is complete, so each call frames new audio only.
      Emitted frames are bit-identical to computeMelSpectrogram's because
      (a) the stream starts and ends with enough zero padding that both
      reflect pads reduce to zeros, (b) a completed window's contents never
      change as more audio arrives, and (c) the spectral tail (melColumns)
      is the offline code operating on identically framed rows, which it
      processes row-independently.
    
    * VoxtralRealtimeAudioEncoder.convStemStep carries causal-conv tails:
      the last kernel-stride = 2 input frames for conv1 (stride 1) and the
      input suffix from the start of the next stride-2 window for conv2
      (1 frame after an even total, 2 after an odd one, preserving the
      downsample phase for arbitrary chunk sizes). Both carries seed with
      the causal zero left-pad, so every produced row sees exactly the
      window the offline convStem sees at the same absolute index.
    
    The offline computeMelSpectrogram / convStem paths are untouched.
    
    * Make the Voxtral Realtime streaming front end O(1) per chunk
    
    Wire VoxtralRealtimeStreamSession to the incremental mel/conv-stem
    primitives instead of re-running convStemForAudio over the whole buffer
    each step. Per step the session now: moves newly fed samples into the
    mel stream, converts completed mel windows to conv rows, and feeds rows
    below the freeze point to the transformer exactly as before.
    
    Exactness argument (same tokens at temperature 0 as the previous
    full-recompute at every step):
    
    * Everything entering carried state is final. Mel windows are emitted
      only once complete, so no window overlapping the still-growing end of
      the stream is ever cached; the causal convs then make each produced
      row depend only on earlier rows.
    
    * The emit ceiling is unchanged. Before finish() the old ceiling was
      min(nAudioTotal, realRegion - guard); prepareMel's right pad always
      spans at least (0 + 1) + 10 tokens past realRegion, so the clamp never
      bound and realRegion - guard is the same value. The frozen conv rows
      it exposes need mel windows ending at least a guard token before the
      end of real audio, so they are always available without any right
      padding (the guard margin exceeds the 240 trailing samples a window
      can await).
    
    * finish() appends the exact right pad prepareMel would have used
      (token alignment + delay tokens of zeros) plus the 40 zero samples the
      offline path reads from the trailing reflect pad of that zero region,
      reproducing the offline frame count and tail rows exactly.
    
    prepareMel's odd-mel-frame drop and convStem's leading downsample
    truncation are both no-ops for whole-token streams (frame counts are
    multiples of 8), which is the only kind the session ever frames.
    
    Also drops the retained full realAudio buffer (a sample count suffices)
    and the per-step full-buffer reflect-pad CPU loop and GPU->CPU->GPU
    copies that came with it. step() after finish() is now ignored: the
    final pad seals the stream (previously it silently corrupted the
    already-consumed encoder state).
    
    * Test streaming front-end equivalence against the offline pipeline
    
    Feeds a deterministic sine sweep in irregular chunk sizes (100 ms, odd
    runts, 1.7 s, single samples, 480 ms) and asserts zero difference:
    
    * mel: VoxtralRealtimeMelStream vs computeMelSpectrogram over the same
      zero-padded stream, including the finish() tail pad;
    * conv rows: convStemStep chained on the streamed mel vs convStem on
      the offline mel (random-initialized encoder, seeded);
    * end to end: session tokens/text vs generate() at temperature 0 on a
      tiny random-weight fixture (weights actually depend on the conv rows,
      unlike the existing all-zero EOS fixture);
    * window bookkeeping: emitted frame counts for sub-hop feeds.
    
    All weight-free (no model download) and CPU-friendly.
    
    * Match offline transcription for empty-audio finish; broaden equivalence tests
    
    finish() with zero prior real samples previously returned an empty Delta
    without ever appending the final pad, while offline generate() over an
    empty buffer still transcribes the zero-padded empty stream (left pad +
    alignment + delay tokens). Let a final advance() through the no-audio
    guard so the finish pad alone drives the pipeline; the degenerate case
    now produces exactly the offline token sequence. Double-finish and
    step()-after-finish stay sealed by the existing flushed guard (the pad
    is appended at most once, later audio is dropped).
    
    New equivalence cases, same zero-tolerance bar as the existing suite:
    - whole utterance in a single step() then finish()
    - step() after finish() is ignored (text/tokens unchanged)
    - double finish() is a no-op
    - non-zero transcriptionDelayMs (960 ms) end to end vs offline
    - one real encoder transformer layer with 144 conv rows, crossing the
      64-frame sliding window twice, so feedIncremental's cache-reset path
      is compared against encodeChunked with actual attention in play
    - empty-audio finish() equals offline (locks in the fix above)
    
    * Fix the mel-stream left-pad precondition boundary; fix a truncation comment
    
    The offline leading reflect pad mirrors stream indices 1...windowSize/2, so
    a left pad of exactly windowSize/2 puts the first real sample inside the
    mirrored region and the zero-seeded carry would be wrong. Require a strict
    inequality (unreachable via the session, whose pads are whole tokens).
    Also: convStem's % downsampleFactor truncation drops leading rows, not
    trailing ones.
    
    * Allow kernel-level float drift in the mel/conv equivalence tests; tighten comments
    
    The streamed slabs present bit-identical sample windows, but MLX kernels are
    not guaranteed per-row deterministic across batch shapes: CI's virtualized
    Metal shows ~2e-4 log-mel / ~7e-6 conv drift that hardware Metal does not.
    Compare against tight epsilons instead of zero and say so in the header.
    Token-level comparisons stay exact.

